BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2026-10-09-computational-phenotypes@edm-megalab.emory.edu
DTSTAMP:20261029T120000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260925T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260925T133000
SUMMARY:Computational phenotypes: traits\, states\, or moving targets? — 
 Emory Decision-Making Megalab
DESCRIPTION:Article discussion. At our September 25 meeting\, we discussed 
 individual differences\, repeated measurement\, parameter stability\, and 
 what makes a useful computational phenotype\, drawing on Schurr et al. (20
 24) and the companion paper by Katahira\, Oba & Toyama (2024). Featured ar
 ticle: Dynamic computational phenotyping of human cognition. Authors: Schu
 rr\, Reznik\, Hillman\, Bhui\, et al.. Journal: Nature Human Behaviour (20
 24). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-024-01814-x Featured article: Does the
  reliability of computational models truly improve with hierarchical model
 ing? Some recommendations and considerations for the assessment of model p
 arameter reliability. Authors: Katahira\, Oba & Toyama. Journal: Psychonom
 ic Bulletin & Review (2024). https://doi.org/10.3758/s13423-024-02490-8 Le
 ad: Michael Hallquist. Location: PAIS 280.
LOCATION:PAIS 280
URL:https://hallquistlab.github.io/edm_megalab/meetings/2026-09-25/
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2026-09-11-community-kickoff@edm-megalab.emory.edu
DTSTAMP:20261029T120000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261009T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261009T133000
SUMMARY:What should EDM Megalab be? — Emory Decision-Making Megalab
DESCRIPTION:Community planning meeting. Everyone in the Emory community wit
 h an interest in decision-making is welcome. Building on the broad partici
 pation at our September 25 meeting\, we will get to know one another\, map
  shared interests\, methods\, datasets\, and opportunities\, and shape pri
 orities for the series. Join the conversation whether you attended last ti
 me or are new to EDM Megalab.  Lead: Michael Hallquist. Location: PAIS 280
 .
LOCATION:PAIS 280
URL:https://hallquistlab.github.io/edm_megalab/meetings/2026-10-09/
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2026-10-23-fmri-preprocessing@edm-megalab.emory.edu
DTSTAMP:20261029T120000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261023T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261023T133000
SUMMARY:WIP presentations — Emory Decision-Making Megalab
DESCRIPTION:Works in progress. Works-in-progress presentations from Pete Hi
 tchcock on a decision-science approach to negative self-judgment in depres
 sion and Michael Hallquist on learning what to choose and when to act\, co
 nnecting response vigor\, matching\, and free-operant behavior\, with time
  for discussion and feedback. Background papers for both presentations are
  linked with this meeting. No advance reading is required. Presentations: 
 Pete Hitchcock: Making Progress on Inherently Complex ("Low Essentiality")
  Disorders: A Case Study. Hitchcock introduces the idea that mental health
  problems vary in their inherent complexity and argues that depression and
  generalized anxiety are inherently complex\, or low in essentiality. As a
  case study in making progress on these problems\, he describes the lab’
 s decision-science approach to negative self-judgment in depression. This 
 work builds on sequential-sampling models\, in which decisions unfold by a
 ccumulating evidence over time: visual evidence for perceptual judgments\,
  such as identifying a traffic light’s color\, and personal value associ
 ations for food choices\, such as choosing between Butterfingers and Reese
 ’s.\; Michael Hallquist: Learning when to act and what to choose. Hallqu
 ist explores how learning shapes both action choice and response timing\, 
 connecting response vigor with the matching law. He compares computational
  models across reward schedules\, extinction\, changing reward environment
 s\, and tasks that reward waiting. The talk highlights where the models su
 cceed and fall short\, and proposes human studies to test their potential 
 relevance to anhedonia\, effort-related inactivity\, and impulsive action.
 . Optional background: Decision Making and Sequential Sampling from Memory
 . Authors: Shadlen & Shohamy. Journal: Neuron (2016). Shared by Pete Hitch
 cock: proposes that value-based choices accumulate evidence retrieved from
  memory\, extending sequential-sampling accounts of perceptual decisions. 
 https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4891701/ Optional background: The
  hippocampus supports deliberation during value-based decisions. Authors: 
 Bakkour\, Palombo\, Zylberberg\, Kang\, Reid\, Verfaellie\, Shadlen & Shoh
 amy. Journal: eLife (2019). Shared by Pete Hitchcock: fMRI and evidence fr
 om patients with hippocampal damage support a role for the hippocampus in 
 deliberation during value-based choices. https://elifesciences.org/article
 s/46080 Background reading: Tonic dopamine: Opportunity costs and the cont
 rol of response vigor. Authors: Niv\, Daw\, Joel & Dayan. Journal: Psychop
 harmacology (2007). Background on average reward rate\, the opportunity co
 st of time\, and response vigor. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17031711/
  Background reading: On two types of deviation from the matching law: Bias
  and undermatching. Authors: Baum. Journal: Journal of the Experimental An
 alysis of Behavior (1974). Background on how behavior is allocated across 
 competing sources of reinforcement. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/
 PMC1333261/ Optional background: A comparison of variable-ratio and variab
 le-interval schedules of reinforcement. Authors: Zuriff. Journal: Journal 
 of the Experimental Analysis of Behavior (1970). Shows how different reinf
 orcement contingencies can produce different response rates even at equal 
 reinforcement rates. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1333678/ Lea
 d: Pete Hitchcock and Michael Hallquist. Location: PAIS 280.
LOCATION:PAIS 280
URL:https://hallquistlab.github.io/edm_megalab/meetings/2026-10-23/
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2026-10-30-don-robinaugh@edm-megalab.emory.edu
DTSTAMP:20261029T120000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261030T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261030T133000
SUMMARY:Advancing cognitive behavioral theories and therapies with computat
 ional models — Emory Decision-Making Megalab
DESCRIPTION:External speaker. Don Robinaugh from Northeastern will give an 
 EDM Zoom talk. Watch together in PAIS 280. Abstract: Cognitive behavioral 
 theories of mental disorders have received considerable empirical support.
  However\, these theories rarely develop over time in a way that would ind
 icate a substantial accumulation of knowledge. Perhaps not surprisingly\, 
 then\, the efficacy of cognitive behavioral treatments for many disorders 
 has not improved in decades. In this talk\, I will argue that formalizing 
 cognitive behavioral theories as computational models is one means by whic
 h we might better advance our theories and our treatments. I will illustra
 te how computational models equip us to more accurately and precisely dedu
 ce what our theories predict\, and how these advantages can be applied to 
 advance our understanding of how treatments work\, why they sometimes fail
 \, and how they might be improved. Finally\, I will discuss the challenges
  facing this work and the importance of integrating formalized clinical th
 eory with computational models from other areas of psychology and psychiat
 ry.  Speaker: Don Robinaugh (Northeastern). Speaker profile: https://schol
 ar.google.com/citations?user=Dp0_nY0AAAAJ&hl=en&oi=ao. Location: PAIS 280 
 · Zoom watch party.
LOCATION:PAIS 280 · Zoom watch party
URL:https://hallquistlab.github.io/edm_megalab/meetings/2026-10-30/
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
